  "description": "Faithful replication with the original renderings, for 3 of the paper's 9 viewpoints: rendered views of three public sites in Daejeon, South Korea (DO1 Daejeon Observatory, HH2 Hoedeok Hyanggyo, NJ3 Namgan Jeongsa), each in the paper's three matched greenery conditions built in SketchUp and rendered in Lumion: Reduced, Modeled reference and Added vegetation (visible vegetation 8.0 / 16.5 / 23.7% for DO1, 21.6 / 43.2 / 67.9% for HH2, 20.1 / 38.1 / 58.0% for NJ3). Participants see the 9 scenes in individually randomized order, as in the paper, and after each scene answer, with the scene still shown, the paper's eight perceived-restorativeness statements (e.g. I feel restored after viewing here; Viewing here helps me reduce stress), its eight Perceived Sensory Dimension statements (social, space, nature, refuge, prospect, serene, culture, rich in species) on 5-point scales (1 = strongly disagree, 5 = strongly agree) and its two scene-preference items (How attractive did you find the scene?, 1 = not at all, 5 = extremely attractive; Do you want to visit here?, 1 = not at all, 5 = extremely want). Gender and age are asked at the end. Rebuilt from Lee, Hsu, Yang & Lee (2026), Visible Greenery and Visual Composition in Urban Views: A Matched-Scene Experiment on Restorativeness and Preference, Buildings 16(19): 3777, with the renderings cropped from its Figure 5 (the visible-vegetation labels trimmed off) and all items from its Tables 2-4 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: viewpoint_condition, e.g. DO1_reduced, HH2_reference, NJ3_added. Analysis as in the paper: perceived-restorativeness score (mean of 8 items) and preference score (mean of 2 items) per scene, compared between Reduced and Modeled reference and between Added and Modeled reference with linear models containing participant and viewpoint fixed effects and standard errors clustered by participant and viewpoint, and each Perceived Sensory Dimension compared the same way; the paper (114 participants, 27 scenes) found Reduced scenes rated lower in restorativeness (b = -0.278) and preference (b = -0.289) than the Modeled reference, while Added scenes did not reliably improve either; Added scenes were rated higher on nature and rich in species but lower on prospect. Differences from the original study: (1) only the 3 viewpoints whose clean renderings are printed (Figure 5) are used, 9 of the 27 scenes; the other 18 are printed only with segmentation overlays (Figure A1); (2) the paper's web interface ran on personal computers only and recruited through university-affiliated online platforms; here any device can be used and anyone can take part; (3) the instruction text and the gender and age questions are ours; the paper reports age groups 20-29, 30-39 and 40-49; (4) the renderings are the figure copies (about 1600 px wide) with the top edge trimmed.",
